LAN: local area network. It is what is connected to a router interface.

At layer 2 the data being transmitted is called a frame (which is a packet (L3) to which were added a header and trailer). The most common frames are Ethernet frames.

What's in the frame header: preamble (7 bytes -> 10101010 * 7 which allows receivers to synchronize their clocks), start frame delimiter (SFD) (1 byte -> 10101011 which
indicates the end of preamble and start of the rest of the frame), destination (L2 address to be sent to (MAC address 6 bytes)), source (L2 adress who sent (MAC adress 6
bytes)), and type (L3 protocol used in the encapsulated packet (IPV4 or IPV6)). The type can be replaced sometimes by the lenght field which indicates the lenght of
encapsulated data. The last field has 2 byte, if the value is 1500 or less, it means it's the lenght, if it is 1536 or more, it means the type.

What's in the frame trailer: there's only the frame check sequence (FCS) which is used by the receiver to check if there's any error using CRC (cyclic redundancy check) 
(similar to hash). It has 4 bytes.

When different devices are connected to a switch interface, the switch doesn't know which MAC address is associated with which interface. It has to discover it when a device
wants to send data to another device. \the process is as follow. The device sends a frame to the switch. The switch looks at it's MAC address table, if the source MAC address
is not in the table, it will add it. If the destination MAC address is not in the table (unknow unicast frame) it will send the frame to all the devices connected to its
interface (flood the network).On the other hand, if the MAC address becomes known in the future (known unicast frame), it will only send the frame to the corresponding
interface. On Cisco switches, MAC addressed are removed after 5min of incativity. This whole process is called Dynamic MAC address.
